# Functions

## FUNC-01 — Do one thing

A function must do exactly one thing, do it well, and do it only. If a function contains steps that are one level of abstraction below its name, it is doing more than one thing.

A reliable test: can you extract a meaningful sub-function from its body with a name that is not merely a restatement of its implementation? If yes, the function is doing too much.

```
// bad — queries, transforms, and writes in one function
processOrder(order):
    validate(order)
    total = sum(order.lines)
    tax = total * TAX_RATE
    db.save({ ...order, total, tax })
    email.send(order.user, "Order confirmed")

// good — each function has one job; caller orchestrates
submitOrder(order):
    validated = validateOrder(order)
    priced = applyPricing(validated)
    saveOrder(priced)
    notifyUser(priced)
```

## FUNC-02 — Use descriptive names

A function name must tell you what it does without requiring you to read its body. Long descriptive names are better than short cryptic ones or a long comment.

```
// bad
process(x)
handle()
doStuff(user)

// good
calculateInvoiceTotal(lineItems)
sendPasswordResetEmail(user)
filterActiveQuestions(questions)
```

## FUNC-03 — Prefer fewer arguments

The ideal number of arguments is zero. One is good. Two is acceptable. Three or more requires strong justification. Many arguments usually signal that some of them belong together in a value object.

```
// bad — six primitives; caller must remember order
createUser(firstName, lastName, email, role, orgId, isActive)

// good — group related data; caller is self-documenting
createUser(UserDetails { firstName, lastName, email }, orgId, role)
```

## FUNC-04 — No flag arguments

Do not pass a boolean flag to a function to switch its behaviour. A flag argument is a declaration that the function does two things. Split it into two independent functions.

```
// bad — what does true mean here?
render(widget, true)
render(widget, false)

// good — intent is explicit at every call site
renderExpanded(widget)
renderCollapsed(widget)
```

## FUNC-05 — No side effects

A function must do only what its name promises. Hidden side effects — mutating global state, writing to a file, sending an event — violate the single-responsibility contract and make functions unpredictable.

```
// bad — name says "check", but it also resets the session
isSessionValid(session):
    if expired(session):
        session.reset()   // hidden side effect
        return false
    return true

// good — separate the check from the action
isSessionExpired(session): return expired(session)

// caller decides what to do
if isSessionExpired(session):
    resetSession(session)
```

## FUNC-06 — Clarity over cleverness

Code is read far more than it is written. Every construct must be immediately understandable without mental parsing. Prefer the straightforward solution even when a shorter, cleverer one exists.

```
// bad — three separate iterations over the same collection
results = items
    .filter(active)
    .map(toQuestion)
    .find(matching)

// good — single pass; intent is explicit
index = buildIndex(questions)   // Map: id → question
results = []
for item in items:
    if not item.active: continue
    question = index.get(item.id)
    if question: results.append(question)
```

## FUNC-07 — No duplicated logic

Never write the same logic in two or more places. If behaviour is repeated, extract it to a single shared function and call it from both sites. Duplication means two places to change when requirements change, and two ways for them to diverge.

```
// bad — same validation written twice
createUser(data):
    if not data.email or '@' not in data.email:
        raise ValidationError("Invalid email")
    ...

updateUser(data):
    if not data.email or '@' not in data.email:
        raise ValidationError("Invalid email")
    ...

// good — one definition, two call sites
validateEmail(email):
    if not email or '@' not in email:
        raise ValidationError("Invalid email")

createUser(data): validateEmail(data.email) ...
updateUser(data): validateEmail(data.email) ...
```

## FUNC-08 — Existence check before value check

When inspecting an optional value, check for its existence before comparing its content. Checking content first can misclassify a valid falsy value (zero, false, empty string) as absent.

```
// bad — value check before existence; misclassifies 0 as absent
if value != 0 and not value: ...

// good — existence first, then content
if value is not None and value != 0: ...
```
